{"step_index":33,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:19:21Z","content":"--- **Types of Pain** • **Somatic Pain** • Arises from noxious stimulation of musculoskeletal cutaneous and deep somatic structures • Superficial Somatic Originates in skin or subcutaneous tissues sharp pricking well localized with clearly defined boundaries • Deep Somatic Originates in muscles tendons ligaments periosteum and joint capsules dull aching poorly localized and tends to refer to related sclerotomal structures • **Somatic Referred Pain** • Pain perceived at a site distant from the actual primary site of somatic injury or dysfunction eg cervical facet joint dysfunction referring into the shoulder girdle • Follows an embryological segmental or sclerotomal pattern without mechanical irritation or compression of neural tissue • Characterized by a diffuse deep ache with no primary neurological signs normal reflexes sensation and motor power • **Neurogenic Neuropathic Pain** • Initiated or caused by a primary lesion dysfunction or mechanical irritation of the peripheral or central nervous system • Described as sharp shooting lancinating burning stabbing or electric shock like sensations • Follows discrete anatomical distributions dermatomal tracts or peripheral nerve distributions and frequently presents with positive or negative neurological symptoms paresthesia hyperalgesia allodynia hypesthesia motor deficits • **Visceral Pain** • Arises from the distension ischemia spasm or inflammation of internal organs thoracic abdominal or pelvic • Poorly localized deep sickening dull and cramp like • Frequently accompanied by autonomic manifestations nausea diaphoresis pallor blood pressure changes and visceral somatic referral patterns eg myocardial ischemia referring to the jaw/left arm gallbladder referral to the right inferior scapular region --- **Subjective Assessment of Pain** • **Location** • Pinpointing the exact anatomical site and boundary using a standardized body chart distinguishing localized pain from widespread or multi site pain • **Duration** • Timeframe since the onset of symptoms • Acute Less than 6 weeks direct relation to tissue injury and healing • Subacute 6 to 12 weeks • Chronic / Persistent Greater than 3 to 6 months outlasting normal physiological healing time • **Progression** • The chronological course of the pain over time improving worsening static intermittent episodic or recurrent • **Distribution** • Spatial pattern of symptoms focal segmental/sclerotomal radiating along a dermatomal/peripheral nerve track or non anatomical/diffuse • **Quality** • Subjective descriptive character of the sensation eg sharp dull ache burning throbbing heavy stabbing indicating the underlying tissue source and neurophysiological mechanism • **Diurnal Variations** • Behavior of symptoms across a 24 hour day night cycle • Morning Stiffness and pain lasting more than 30 to 60 minutes points to inflammatory etiology eg rheumatoid arthritis • Evening / End of day Pain aggravated by weight bearing and activity points to mechanical or degenerative etiology eg osteoarthritis • Night Constant unremitting pain waking the patient from sound sleep is a clinical red flag requiring evaluation for non mechanical pathology eg malignancy infection • **Modifying Factors** • Aggravating Factors Positions functional movements postures or sustained loads that provoke or intensify the pain • Easing Factors Positions of relief rest thermal modalities or medications that alleviate the pain • **Severity** • Magnitude or intensity of the pain assessed at present at its best and at its worst over a defined period eg last 24 hours • **Nature of Pain** • Underlying clinical mechanism generating the symptoms • Mechanical Pain varies predictably with physical loads movements and rest • Inflammatory Constant aching marked morning stiffness warmth throbbing eased slightly by gentle movement • Neuropathic Radicular burning electric sensations with neurosensory deficits • Nociplastic / Central Sensitization Disproportionate pain intensity with hypersensitivity and non mechanical triggers • **Tissue Irritability** • Clinical concept Maitland determining how easily symptoms are provoked and how readily they settle dictating safe physical examination depth and treatment vigor • Vigor/Threshold The amount and intensity of activity required to bring on the pain • Severity The peak intensity of the provoked pain • Latency / Time to Ease The time required for pain to return to baseline once the provoking activity stops • Triage **High irritability** provoked easily severe long time to ease requires gentle handling **Moderate irritability** moderate load needed moderate intensity eases in minutes **Low irritability** high load required mild pain eases immediately --- **Objective Measurement and Documentation** • **Visual Analogue Scale VAS** • Format A continuous horizontal unnumbered line exactly 100 mm 10 cm in length • Anchors Clearly demarcated endpoints labeled No pain 0 mm at the left and Worst pain imaginable 100 mm at the right • Administration The patient marks a single vertical tick across the line representing their current or peak pain level • Measurement and Documentation Measured with a millimeter ruler from the left anchor to the patients mark documented as a continuous value from 0 to 100 mm or 0 to 10 cm Highly sensitive to small clinical changes • **Numerical Rating Scale NRS** • Format An 11 point segmented numerical scale ranging from 0 to 10 • Anchors 0 equals No pain 1 to 3 equals Mild pain 4 to 6 equals Moderate pain 7 to 9 equals Severe pain 10 equals Worst possible pain • Administration Administered either verbally On a scale from 0 to 10 where 0 is no pain and 10 is the worst pain imaginable or visually as a numbered line • Documentation Recorded directly as a discrete integer score eg NRS 6/10 Highly practical for rapid clinical workflows and routine reassessment • **McGills Modified Questionnaire Short Form McGill Pain Questionnaire SF MPQ** • Format A multidimensional pain assessment instrument evaluating sensory affective and evaluative qualities rather than intensity alone • Sensory Component 11 sensory word descriptors throbbing shooting stabbing sharp cramping gnawing hot burning aching heavy tender splitting • Affective Component 4 affective word descriptors tiring exhausting sickening fearful punishing cruel • Scoring System Each of the 15 descriptors is rated by the patient on a 4 point intensity scale • 0 equals None • 1 equals Mild • 2 equals Moderate • 3 equals Severe • Indices Documented • Sensory Pain Rating Index S PRI Sum of sensory items 0 to 33 • Affective Pain Rating Index A PRI Sum of affective items 0 to 12 • Total PRI Combined sum 0 to 45 • Present Pain Intensity PPI A 6 point categorical index 0 equals No pain 1 equals Mild 2 equals Discomforting 3 equals Distressing 4 equals Horrible 5 equals Excruciating • Visual Analogue Scale VAS Standard 100 mm line included alongside the questionnaire for overall pain intensity","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing on the most efficient tool utilization. It's becoming clearer that prioritizing specific tools will drastically improve my problem-solving ability. The goal is to always select the tool best suited for the immediate task at hand, regardless of how broad a command may be. This will keep work on the project organized.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Selection**\n\nI'm now honing my tool selection methodology. Remembering critical instruction one and two, I'm aiming to avoid common pitfalls like using `cat` for file creation and `grep` inside `bash`. I'm working to proactively list potential tool candidates before executing any command. My goal is to ensure only the most appropriate tools are called, keeping my thought process organized and efficient.\n\n\n**Analyzing Text Transformation**\n\nI'm now deep diving into text transformation rules • I've focu