{"step_index":36,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T18:25:26Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n## 3. Modified Medical Research Council (mMRC) Dyspnea Scale\n\n- **Definition:**\n  - The mMRC Dyspnea Scale is a subjective assessment tool used to grade breathlessness according to its effect on daily physical activities.\n\n- **Purpose:**\n  - To assess the severity of dyspnea in respiratory disorders.\n  - To evaluate functional limitation during daily activities.\n  - To monitor disease progression and rehabilitation outcomes.\n  - Commonly used in patients with COPD and other chronic respiratory diseases.\n\n- **Grades of Dyspnea:**\n  - **Grade 0:** Breathlessness occurs only during strenuous exercise.\n  - **Grade 1:** Breathlessness occurs when hurrying on level ground or walking up a slight hill.\n  - **Grade 2:** Walks slower than people of the same age on level ground due to breathlessness or has to stop for breath while walking at own pace.\n  - **Grade 3:** Stops for breath after walking approximately 100 meters or after a few minutes on level ground.\n  - **Grade 4:** Too breathless to leave the house or becomes breathless while dressing or undressing.\n\n- **Clinical Significance:**\n  - Higher grades indicate greater functional limitation due to breathlessness.\n  - Useful in assessing COPD severity and functional disability.\n  - Helps evaluate changes in symptoms during pulmonary rehabilitation.\n\n- **Interpretation:**\n  - Grade 0–1 – Minimal functional limitation.\n  - Grade 2 – Moderate functional limitation.\n  - Grade 3–4 – Severe functional limitation.\n\nSource: National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine.\nhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK593672/\n\n---\n\n## 4. New York Heart Association (NYHA) Functional Classification\n\n- **Definition:**\n  - The NYHA Functional Classification is a clinical assessment tool used to classify patients with cardiovascular disease according to the degree of limitation in physical activity caused by cardiac symptoms.\n\n- **Purpose:**\n  - To assess functional capacity in patients with heart disease.\n  - To determine the severity of symptoms during physical activity.\n  - To monitor the patient's clinical progress.\n  - Commonly used in patients with heart failure.\n\n- **Grades of NYHA Classification:**\n\n- **Class I:**\n  - No limitation of physical activity.\n  - Ordinary physical activity does not cause undue fatigue, palpitation, dyspnea, or chest pain.\n\n- **Class II:**\n  - Slight limitation of physical activity.\n  - Patient is comfortable at rest.\n  - Ordinary physical activity causes fatigue, palpitation, dyspnea, or chest pain.\n\n- **Class III:**\n  - Marked limitation of physical activity.\n  - Patient is comfortable at rest.\n  - Less than ordinary physical activity causes fatigue, palpitation, dyspnea, or chest pain.\n\n- **Class IV:**\n  - Patient is unable to perform any physical activity without discomfort.\n  - Symptoms may be present even at rest.\n  - Any physical activity increases discomfort.\n\n- **Clinical Significance:**\n  - Helps assess the functional status of patients with cardiovascular disorders.\n  - Useful in cardiac rehabilitation and exercise prescription.\n  - Helps monitor improvement or deterioration in functional capacity.\n  - Higher class indicates greater limitation in physical activity.\n\nSource: American Heart Association.\nhttps://www.heart.org/en/health-topics/heart-failure/what-is-heart-failure/classes-of-heart-failure\n\n---\n\n## 5. Difference Between mMRC and NYHA Classification\n\n- **mMRC Scale:**\n  - Primarily used to assess breathlessness-related functional limitation in respiratory disorders.\n  - Consists of 5 grades: 0–4.\n  - Focuses mainly on breathlessness during daily activities.\n\n- **NYHA Classification:**\n  - Primarily used to assess functional limitation in cardiovascular disorders, especially heart failure.\n  - Consists of 4 classes: I–IV.\n  - Focuses on symptoms such as dyspnea, fatigue, palpitation, and chest pain during physical activity.\n\n- Both scales are subjective and should be interpreted alongside clinical examination and other functional assessments.\n\n\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-17T23:55:26+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
